{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now supports R7g database instances in additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now supports R7g database instances in several new AWS regions, including AWS GovCloud (US-East), Asia Pacific (Osaka), Europe (Zurich), Asia Pacific (Jakarta), Israel (Tel Aviv), and Canada West (Calgary). These instances are based on AWS Graviton3 processors, which offer a 30% performance improvement over Graviton2-based instances. R7g instances are the first to feature DDR5 memory, which provides 50% more memory bandwidth than DDR4","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/07/amazon-rds-postgresql-mysql-mariadb-r7g/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now supports M7g and R7g database instances in additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now supports M7g and R7g database instances in several new AWS Regions, including Asia Pacific (Jakarta), Middle East (UAE), South America (Sao Paulo), Asia Pacific (Osaka), Asia Pacific (Melbourne), Israel (Tel Aviv), Europe (Zurich), AWS GovCloud (US-East), Middle East (Bahrain), South America (Sao Paulo), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). These instances are based on Graviton3 processors, which offer a 30% performance improvement over Graviton2-based…","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/05/amazon-rds-postgresql-mysql-mariadb-m7g-r7g/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon Aurora and RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB now support r8g, r7g, and r7i database instances in additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon Aurora and Relational Database Service (RDS) for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB now support new database instance types, r8g, r7g, and r7i, in additional AWS regions. R8g instances are available in Asia Pacific (Hong Kong, Osaka, Jakarta, Seoul, Singapore, and Canada Central), R7i in Asia Pacific (Hyderabad), and R7g in Africa (Cape Town). These instances are based on AWS Graviton4 processors and offer up to 40% performance improvement and up to 29% price/performance improvement over Graviton3-based instances. R8g instances come in 24xlarge and 48xlarge sizes, delivering up to 192 vCPUs, an 8:1 ratio of memory to vCPU with DDR5 memory, enhanced networking bandwidth of up to 50Gbps, and up to 40Gbps bandwidth to Amazon Elastic Block Store. R7g instances offer up to 128 vCPUs and up to 384GB of memory. R8g and R7g instances are available for Amazon Aurora with MySQL compatibility and Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MariaDB. For specific engine versions and pricing information, refer to the Aurora and RDS documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/amazon-aurora-rds-r8g-r7g-r7i-instances-additional-regions/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon Aurora now supports R7g database instances in additional AWS Regions - AWS","context":"Amazon Aurora now supports R7g database instances with Graviton3 processors in additional AWS regions, including Asia Pacific (Osaka), Asia Pacific (Jakarta), Canada West (Calgary), Europe (Zurich), Europe (Paris), Israel (Tel Aviv), Middle East (UAE), and AWS GovCloud (US-East). These instances offer up to 30% performance improvement over Graviton2 instances and are the first to feature DDR5 memory, which provides 50% more memory bandwidth. R7g instances offer enhanced networking bandwidth of up to 30Gbps and up to 20 Gbps to Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS). They are supported by Aurora MySQL version 3.03.1 and higher, and Aurora PostgreSQL version 13.10 and higher, 14.7 and higher, and 15.2 and higher. Upgrading to Graviton3 requires a simple instance type modification. Amazon Aurora, designed for high performance and availability at global scale, offers built-in security, continuous backups, serverless compute, up to 15 read replicas, automated multi-Region replication, and integrations with other AWS services. To get started, refer to the getting started page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/07/amazon-aurora-r7g/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"rds-oss","skill_description":"Advises on Amazon RDS open-source engines (MySQL, MariaDB, PostgreSQL) for instance creation, upgrade planning, commitment pricing, proxy evaluation, and Blue/Green deployments. Handles any RDS MySQL, MariaDB, or PostgreSQL question, including create a production-ready RDS MySQL instance, provision an RDS PostgreSQL database, run the RDS upgrade advisor for my RDS MySQL instance, what are my upgrade options, upgrade RDS MariaDB from 10.6 to the latest version, should I buy reserved instances or a savings plan for db.r7g.2xlarge RDS MySQL, change a VARCHAR to INT column on RDS MySQL 8.0 with Blue/Green, and does RDS Proxy help when PgBouncer already runs in transaction mode. Covers instance creation with production best practices, describe-db-instances and describe-db-engine-versions upgrade-target workflow, live prechecks via SSM or direct connection, RI versus DSP commitment pricing, RDS Proxy versus PgBouncer, and Blue/Green lifecycle with binlog replay compatibility.\n\nServices: rds, mysql, mariadb, postgres, rds-proxy, savings-plans\nTasks: advise, analyze, optimize, create, provision, upgrade-planning, commitment-analysis, schema-change\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, dba, finance\nWorkload: database, relational-database\n*","skill_name":"rds-oss"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL Graviton instance class support", "parent_id": "eb6f6eba-8560-4501-8883-ce30a665babd"}

