{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Lambda adds support for Python 3.14 - AWS","context":"AWS Lambda has added support for Python 3.14 as both a managed runtime and a container base image. Developers can utilize the latest Python 3.14 language features with Lambda and Lambda@Edge in supported Regions. Powertools for AWS Lambda, a developer toolkit, also supports Python 3.14. AWS provides various deployment tools, including the Lambda console, AWS CLI, AWS Serverless Application Model (AWS SAM), AWS CDK, and AWS CloudFormation, to deploy and manage serverless applications written in Python 3.14. The Python 3.14 runtime is available in all AWS Regions, including AWS GovCloud (US) and China Regions. For more information on upgrading existing Lambda functions and other details, refer to the blog post and AWS Lambda product page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/aws-lambda-python-314/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Lambda now supports Graviton5-powered EC2 instances on Lambda Managed Instances - AWS","context":"AWS Lambda now supports the use of Graviton5-powered C9g, C9gd, M9g, and M9gd EC2 instances for Lambda Managed Instances. This update allows users to run their Lambda functions on the latest generation of Graviton processors, which delivers up to 25% better compute performance compared to Graviton4-powered instances. Lambda Managed Instances is a service that lets users run Lambda functions on their EC2 instances while maintaining Lambda's operational simplicity. It offers access to specialized compute configurations, cost efficiency through EC2 pricing advantages, and automatic management of infrastructure tasks. Users can specify the desired Graviton5 instance type when creating a capacity provider, and Lambda will automatically include those instances in its selection based on the function's architecture, memory size, and memory-to-vCPU ratio. The Graviton5-powered instances are available in all regions where both Lambda Managed Instances and these EC2 instances are available. To learn more, visit the Lambda Managed Instances documentation and AWS Lambda pricing","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-lambda-graviton5-ec2/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon ECS Express Mode now supports AWS Graviton (ARM64) workloads - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS) Express Mode now supports Amazon Graviton (ARM64) workloads, enabling users to specify ARM64 as the CPU architecture for their services. This new feature allows for the deployment of ARM-based container images on AWS Graviton-powered compute, providing up to 40% better price-performance compared to x86-based instances. Amazon ECS Express Mode simplifies the process of launching containerized applications by managing the cloud architecture, including networking, load balancing, auto scaling, and deployments, and providing an auto-generated URL. Users can configure the CPU architecture for new and existing Amazon ECS Express Mode services using various methods, including the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, and infrastructure as code (IaC) tools. This capability is available in all AWS commercial and GovCloud (US) Regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/amazon-ecs-express-mode-arm-architecture/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-lambda-durable-functions","skill_description":"Builds resilient, long-running, multi-step applications with AWS Lambda durable functions with automatic state persistence, retry logic, and orchestration for long-running executions. Covers the critical replay model, step operations, wait/callback patterns, error handling with saga pattern, testing with LocalDurableTestRunner. Triggers on phrases like lambda durable functions, durable execution, workflow orchestration, state machines, retry/checkpoint patterns, long-running stateful Lambda functions, saga pattern, human-in-the-loop callbacks, reliable serverless applications, context.step, 